  "response": {
    "verdict": "REVISE",
    "summary": "The revised durable outbox is incomplete: pending alerts can be skipped during API failures, and it does not retain the itinerary link required to send the promised ticket link.",
    "findings": [
      {
        "id": "REV-020",
        "severity": "high",
        "path": "PLAN.md — `check_route` steps 1 and 6",
        "evidence": "Step 1 says that when `fetch_cheapest(route)` returns `None` (including network error, 429, empty response, or route backoff), `check_route` exits. Step 6 simultaneously says `flush_pending_alerts` runs unconditionally every cycle. These are incompatible: a pending Telegram alert is not retried while Travelpayouts is unavailable or the route is rate-limited, even if Telegram has recovered. For example, a failed Telegram alert remains pending, then the next API call receives 429; the stated step-1 return prevents the promised flush.",
        "fix": "Make pending-outbox flushing independent of fetching and anomaly detection: invoke it even when the route is skipped or `fetch_cheapest` returns `None`, and define the exact order in `check_route`."
      },
      {
        "id": "REV-021",
        "severity": "high",
        "path": "PLAN.md — `alerts` schema and `flush_pending_alerts`",
        "evidence": "The planned `alerts` row stores only `route_id`, `price`, `depart_date`, `return_date`, status, and creation time. `flush_pending_alerts` rebuilds the message from those stored fields rather than the current API entry, but neither the API `link` nor the `is_itinerary_specific` result is persisted. Consequently, the delivery path cannot include the detected ticket-specific URL; it must fall back to a generic route search, including on the first alert because delivery was moved from claim to flush.",
        "fix": "At claim time, build and persist an immutable alert payload, at minimum `purchase_url`, `is_itinerary_specific`, and the display fields needed for the message. Flush that stored payload, not a reconstructed one based only on the route and dates."
      },
      {
        "id": "REV-022",
        "severity": "medium",
        "path": "PLAN.md — `flush_pending_alerts` retry policy / Telegram error handling",
        "evidence": "Every pending alert is attempted on every cycle with no persisted `next_attempt_at`, attempt count, expiry, or handling of Telegram HTTP 429. A blocked bot, invalid chat ID, or Telegram rate limit produces a request and log entry every 20 seconds indefinitely. If delivery later resumes after a long outage, the daemon can send alerts for tickets whose cached offer has expired; the planned rows do not retain a discovery timestamp or expiry policy.",
        "fix": "Persist retry metadata and honor Telegram `Retry-After` with capped exponential backoff. Store the alert creation/discovery time and define a stale-alert expiry policy; report expired pending rows rather than sending obsolete ticket alerts."
      }
    ],
